#!/sbin/openrc-run
# infra-os:   alpine
# infra-init: openrc
# infra-path: /etc/init.d
# infra-name: cloudflared
# infra-mode: 0755
  
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, AND IT HAS TO BE. Alpine packages no init script for cloudflared —
# docs/host-setup.md installs the binary straight from GitHub — so this was assembled
# from the Alpine package source and other references. Whatever preceded it DID NOT
# START ON REBOOT: the generated version from `cloudflared service install` looks
# correct, starts when started, and is missing the next time the box comes up. Do not
# replace this file with it.
#
# LOGGING, FIXED 2026-08-23. This previously declared stdout_log and stderr_log, which
# supervise-daemon does not act on. /var/log/cloudflared.err was last written on
# 2026-01-04 and cloudflared.log had been 0 bytes since 2025-12-24 — so when six
# consecutive restarts crashed on 2026-08-23 they produced no output whatsoever, and
# the cause had to be found by reproducing it in the foreground instead.
#
# The names below are copied from /etc/init.d/docker: stock Alpine, same supervisor, in
# the default runlevel, demonstrably writing its log. `output_logger` takes a COMMAND
# rather than a path, which is why the old declaration was inert rather than wrong.
# Verified on `one` first, 2026-08-23, before being applied here.

# NO AUTOUPDATE, since 2026-08-23. It was `--autoupdate-freq 24h0m0s` and it was live:
# all three binaries were rewritten on 14-15 August, the day 2026.8.2 was released, on a
# fleet where cloudflared was installed by hand half a year earlier.
#
# Two reasons it is off. First, it replaced a boot-path binary with unverified bytes on a
# 24-hour cycle — the same thing dev/Dockerfile refuses to permit inside a container, on
# the grounds that this binary "holds the credentials that put an ssh endpoint on the
# public internet", and the argument is stronger here, not weaker.
#
# Second, it could never fix what is wrong: cloudflared updates itself with a build
# matching ITS OWN architecture, not the machine's. All three hosts carry byte-identical
# binaries though `two` is armv6 and the others arm64 — so the fleet has been running one
# 32-bit build everywhere since install, and autoupdate perpetuated that indefinitely.
#
# Updates now come from ansible/playbooks/cloudflared-update.yml: right arch per host,
# hash-verified, one host at a time, one -> two -> zero, each stage refusing while an
# earlier one is unhealthy.

cfgfile="/etc/$RC_SVCNAME/$RC_SVCNAME.conf"
supervisor=supervise-daemon
command="/usr/bin/cloudflared"
command_args="--no-autoupdate --config /etc/cloudflared/config.yml tunnel run"
command_user="root"
pidfile="/var/run/$RC_SVCNAME.pid"
command_background="yes"

# `cfgfile` and `command_background` both look vestigial — nothing references either and
# docker's script sets neither. Left alone rather than removed alongside a logging fix.

# ONE file, not two. The split produced a 397 KB .err and a .log empty for eight months,
# because cloudflared writes essentially everything to stderr. `-m` merges them.
CF_LOGFILE="${CF_LOGFILE:-/var/log/${RC_SVCNAME}.log}"
LOGPROXY_OPTS="$LOGPROXY_OPTS -m"

# 0640 rather than docker's 0644 — a deliberate deviation. This service has twice left a
# world-readable file containing its own credential on this fleet; its log does not get
# world-read by default either.
export \
	LOGPROXY_CHMOD="${LOGPROXY_CHMOD:-0640}" \
	LOGPROXY_LOG_DIRECTORY="${LOGPROXY_LOG_DIRECTORY:-/var/log}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SIZE="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SIZE:-104857600}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_TIME="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_TIME:-86400}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SUFFIX="${LOGPROXY_ROTATION_SUFFIX:-.%Y%m%d%H%M%S}" \
	LOGPROXY_ROTATED_FILES="${LOGPROXY_ROTATED_FILES:-5}"

output_logger="log_proxy $LOGPROXY_OPTS $CF_LOGFILE"
error_logger="log_proxy $LOGPROXY_OPTS $CF_LOGFILE"

depend() {
        need net
}
